1104.3784	limitation	Measurement of pulsed magnets, i.e., excitation current changing with time, is hardly covered by the rotating coil method.	Measurement of pulsed magnets, i.e., excitation current changing with time, is hardly covered by the rotating coil method. The measurement of the magnet strength, i.e., the main multipole, is spoiled by the field change over the duration of the coil revolution. Accurate synchronization for the measurement of the excitation current at each angular position could be mandatory to get enough precision. Obviously, increasing the rotation rate of the coil shaft and the bandwidth of the electronics greatly helps and must be taken into account at the design phase of the rotating coils, the highest number of turns for the coil windings is not necessarily the optimum. As an example, the 15 long coil shaft of fig:SM18 currently measures, at 8 turns per second, the field quality of the LHC magnets with high temporal accuracy. The measurement of the field quality, i.e., the higher-order multipoles, is greatly improved {{cite:0927b6382dd600e7e987ec5cb77e2dcb99e837e1}} in this case by the help of a coil scheme to compensate the main multipole (sec:dipcomp and REF ). For fast ramping magnets, it is possible to measure flux variation between zero and nominal current with the harmonic coil fixed at a sufficiently large number of angular positions to perform the Fourier analysis. Experimental work is going on at GSI {{cite:1766af5789d4a92f76943a971863946f9015f690}} for the FAIR project and at CERN for the new Linac quadrupole magnets having a duty cycle of a few milliseconds. This method requires an accurate angle positioning, obtained for instance by a high-resolution stepping motor. It would bring the advantages of the Morgan coil {{cite:480cd7c3d4c94bdf448350a18c3afaf1ee337e23}} technique with a simplified coil frame to be manufactured.	0.9
1104.3784	future_work	Experimental work is going on at GSI {{cite:1766af5789d4a92f76943a971863946f9015f690}} for the FAIR project and at CERN for the new Linac quadrupole magnets having a duty cycle of a few milliseconds.	Measurement of pulsed magnets, i.e., excitation current changing with time, is hardly covered by the rotating coil method. The measurement of the magnet strength, i.e., the main multipole, is spoiled by the field change over the duration of the coil revolution. Accurate synchronization for the measurement of the excitation current at each angular position could be mandatory to get enough precision. Obviously, increasing the rotation rate of the coil shaft and the bandwidth of the electronics greatly helps and must be taken into account at the design phase of the rotating coils, the highest number of turns for the coil windings is not necessarily the optimum. As an example, the 15 long coil shaft of fig:SM18 currently measures, at 8 turns per second, the field quality of the LHC magnets with high temporal accuracy. The measurement of the field quality, i.e., the higher-order multipoles, is greatly improved {{cite:0927b6382dd600e7e987ec5cb77e2dcb99e837e1}} in this case by the help of a coil scheme to compensate the main multipole (sec:dipcomp and REF ). For fast ramping magnets, it is possible to measure flux variation between zero and nominal current with the harmonic coil fixed at a sufficiently large number of angular positions to perform the Fourier analysis. Experimental work is going on at GSI {{cite:1766af5789d4a92f76943a971863946f9015f690}} for the FAIR project and at CERN for the new Linac quadrupole magnets having a duty cycle of a few milliseconds. This method requires an accurate angle positioning, obtained for instance by a high-resolution stepping motor. It would bring the advantages of the Morgan coil {{cite:480cd7c3d4c94bdf448350a18c3afaf1ee337e23}} technique with a simplified coil frame to be manufactured.	0.7
