1106.3736	open_problem	Is it true that any {{formula:0efef31b-5100-40ba-a9fe-429a17072ed4}} -convex open subset of {{formula:beac278a-fcb1-4d28-a208-a74a5f6f30a4}}  is Lefschetz-{{formula:25fd1b48-108b-4148-8f0f-59ed3ea242dc}} -convex?	Is it true that any {{formula:0efef31b-5100-40ba-a9fe-429a17072ed4}} -convex open subset of {{formula:beac278a-fcb1-4d28-a208-a74a5f6f30a4}}  is Lefschetz-{{formula:25fd1b48-108b-4148-8f0f-59ed3ea242dc}} -convex?	0.9
1106.3736	limitation	Note that {{formula:a35e0335-c379-49e3-8b40-0833109aaf90}}  admits no open isometric immersion into a two-convex 1-flat manifold.	Consider an isometric action {{formula:b1877d89-bd40-4cba-9192-f037afcdb733}}  with two fixed points; then take {{formula:0fe61f41-0bd1-4022-bac0-8333932705f4}}  to be the orbit space {{formula:12605943-79b4-4d85-ab84-6264d7e8aadc}}  with singular orbits removed. Note that {{formula:a35e0335-c379-49e3-8b40-0833109aaf90}}  admits no open isometric immersion into a two-convex 1-flat manifold. Hence the two-hull of {{formula:d88d6bc7-3fe7-4293-959b-b1754e9311a7}}  can not be defined in the class of manifolds. On the other hand it can be defined in the class of “Riemannian megafolds”; these creatures were introduced in {{cite:61027ddc2251304281b3c98a2f50cd18ee33afec}} and under a different name in {{cite:9a0ab324a62419f8524c9fd9b71ff9cafcaccd0b}}; they look a lot like Riemannian manifolds, but fail to be topological spaces. (In the above example, the two-hull of {{formula:2719952e-c7be-443a-874e-8c5f2a3aa12f}}  is the Riemannian orbifold {{formula:892fe182-ac1e-4fff-b1b0-2dac09dab511}} .)	0.8
1106.5178	limitation	For these and other use cases, which require the expression of complex media segment information, the current W3C Media Fragments specification is insufficient.	Many annotations concern parts, or segments, of resources rather than the entirety of the resource. While simple segments of resources can be identified and referenced directly using the emerging W3C Media Fragment specification {{cite:b2a432ef54286f58aef08c1cc45be76cd18fe84c}} or media-type-specific fragment identifiers as defined in RFCs, there are many use cases for segments that currently cannot be identified using this proposal. One example is the annotation of historic maps, where users need to draw polygon shapes around the geographic areas they want to address with their notes. Sanderson et al. {{cite:f003a7d19e8c27049b0f2b614a168ec3a375a3cd}} describe another use case where annotators express the relationships between images, texts and other resources in medieval manuscripts by means of line segments. For these and other use cases, which require the expression of complex media segment information, the current W3C Media Fragments specification is insufficient. For this reason, OAC introduces the so-called oac:ConstraintTarget node (CT-1), which constrains the annotation target to a specific segment that is further described by an oac:Constraint (C-1) node. The description of the constraint depends on the application scenario and on the (media) type of the annotated target resource. For example, an SVG path could be used to describe a region within an image.	0.9
