1109.0217	open_problem	The authors in {{cite:ccc6eed5adc050000ac3302498ce54acd48c4f02}} have also applied a variant of the proposed PDE model to the challenging problem of composed segmentation in {{cite:d4334c529ddac329280f99511c9290f94e7746ef}}.	open problem. The authors in {{cite:ccc6eed5adc050000ac3302498ce54acd48c4f02}} have also applied a variant of the proposed PDE model to the challenging problem of composed segmentation in {{cite:d4334c529ddac329280f99511c9290f94e7746ef}}. Besides the methods above, there were some initial work by using wavelets or tight-frames to do texture classification and segmentation {{cite:42b58bb11fa8b766d4a924236f1ac5df2f5e437d}}, {{cite:f2b75031666b49934baebd14ec5d7d3128460b62}}. The tight-frame approach is a versatile and effective tool for many different applications in image processing, see {{cite:5702dafd3c859f0128138034c4e2ecf2ecb280f6}}, {{cite:c3921281419bec53d71201994bd4ffdeef21c52f}}, {{cite:52e1d42d34d3ce272813a5e78faf51c69222140f}}, {{cite:d134f2fc66ccbb7e5664a8520d4004276f8ac7df}}, {{cite:b75f78edc782507d8b985fa6f2740f87178d68f8}}, {{cite:dc54c5a30fec1f0f0b5d72da2a194451da8dd39d}}, {{cite:0855442387036cdd47405a922ccef2918b44d04c}}. There are many kinds of tight-frame systems, such as those from framelets {{cite:5bfa6619471683f29816ea5c861289c8d0129f71}}, contourlets {{cite:8ff8202cf06b7348d6d72420dfb0bf22fe632453}} and curvelets {{cite:8f872a5bd21d4470ec8181c72715bbad65010e81}}, {{cite:f28c2661ddecc60ae1e2dbed090d143b09c5e77f}}, etc. Recently, the authors in {{cite:79b92d23e3d762f5af8df282ac2c4c94d38f13fb}} proposed to combine the tight-frame image restoration model of {{cite:2da2b8fecfd98d13baf833e0c5db08f229676e2d}} and the total variation based segmentation model of {{cite:589470fed1a4c2e1a7675f01ec7134c7d81f82b4}}, {{cite:42c112594b7c749dae038d77e6c40423994783fb}}, {{cite:c247ca6d406f947b396a9e425f67b421c9e99860}} to do segmentation. Their approach results in a minimization problem. In this paper, we also derive a segmentation algorithm that uses the tight-frames. However our method is not based on minimizing any variational model and hence it is different from the method in {{cite:79b92d23e3d762f5af8df282ac2c4c94d38f13fb}}. In fact, our algorithm iteratively updates the set of possible boundary pixels to change the given image into a binary image. Like the method in {{cite:ccc6eed5adc050000ac3302498ce54acd48c4f02}}, our method also has the ability to segment twisted, convoluted and occluded structures. We will show that our algorithm is convergent. In fact, numerical results show that it converges within 10 iterations for 2D as well as 3D Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA) images. We will see that our method can extract much more details from the given image than the method in {{cite:c247ca6d406f947b396a9e425f67b421c9e99860}}, {{cite:79b92d23e3d762f5af8df282ac2c4c94d38f13fb}} and {{cite:ccc6eed5adc050000ac3302498ce54acd48c4f02}}. We remark that a preliminary version of our segmentation algorithm has been given in our proceeding paper {{cite:e59fe9ec00ba1d4f46a5686ac323fe32710c66e8}}. The main contributions in this paper are: (1) a simpler strategy to initialize and refine the regions enclosing the possible boundary of the vessels, (2) the new strategy leads to a simple proof of convergence and easier choice of parameters, (3) a different tight-frame with better directionally selective property is used to obtain more details in the image, (4) a new 3D result with higher noise and more complicated vessel is added. The rest of the paper is organized as follows. In Section , we recall some basic facts about tight-frame and tight-frame algorithms. Our segmentation algorithm is given in Section . In Section we test our algorithm on various real 2D and 3D MRA images and compare it with those representative algorithms from different approaches: PDE-based {{cite:ccc6eed5adc050000ac3302498ce54acd48c4f02}}, tight-frame {{cite:79b92d23e3d762f5af8df282ac2c4c94d38f13fb}} and active contour models {{cite:c247ca6d406f947b396a9e425f67b421c9e99860}}. Conclusions are given in Section .	0.8
1109.0470	future_work	We will proceed now to examine whether it is feasible to implement this methodology for the case of Strange attractors which possess chaotic flows in \(\mathbb{R}^3\).	All in all Flow decomposition for the transient DHO has three immediate implications for the DHO: 1. Existence of Integrable Harmonic oscillator associated with a periodic orbit 2. Localization of the DHO evolution and asymptotic fixed point state 3. Quality factor for DHO as a measure of its energy loss rate and in effect of the damping strenth. We will proceed now to examine whether it is feasible to implement this methodology for the case of Strange attractors which possess chaotic flows in \(\mathbb{R}^3\).	0.9
