1202.2591	future_work	future work, including tying in to Homotopy Type Theory (in the sense of {{cite:0dac6ba5791631780ab426c0a70bba405bd70f8b}} and {{cite:847e79ffe6d3ad0200d6ef07f208fa20819e96d4}}) and other projects.	future work, including tying in to Homotopy Type Theory (in the sense of {{cite:0dac6ba5791631780ab426c0a70bba405bd70f8b}} and {{cite:847e79ffe6d3ad0200d6ef07f208fa20819e96d4}}) and other projects. We use the notation {{formula:9ba977e7-6be4-4cdb-b33f-8a484e13716b}} to denote a set with one element. Given any category {{formula:1bc0ce51-7a52-4133-b917-34da79847a4c}} , we denote the category of all functors {{formula:899f5e7d-582c-439c-9327-c96db539c19e}} by {{formula:fcf06dff-fc4d-4407-bc9c-ea045cdc9ebc}} . The terminal object in {{formula:6b70d36c-520b-4fb2-aed1-8749456b3bd1}} sends each object in {{formula:361332ad-c0ab-4c9c-9f41-5ada7e8545cb}} to {{formula:e5c8848a-3c5f-4ac1-bab7-82edcee2a3eb}} ; we denote it by {{formula:048d2fb2-299c-449c-b7d1-68769648b49e}} . We use {{formula:e85858a6-5256-4860-8794-8d614b134aa2}} to denote a discrete category with one object. For any category {{formula:cb9d390b-d5d4-4500-9552-3a63d204674f}} , there is a one-to-one correspondence between the objects in {{formula:ec3b0c34-4be3-4c2c-893a-dd46cde31746}} and the functors {{formula:0da52aea-5388-4429-8b5a-81737ba9dd77}} . Thus we may denote an object {{formula:af1e10a3-4f22-4f8a-af03-f9a3c42912fe}} by a functor {{formula:a8bd7994-6745-4c3e-9cf9-5aba6da8dfa5}} . In particular, we elide the difference between a set and a functor {{formula:f1852e05-7b55-47f2-a4f1-af3f6fbb2bba}} . We draw schemas in one of two ways. When trying to save space, we draw our objects as concisely-labeled nodes and our morphisms as concisely-labeled arrows; when trying to be more expressive, we draw our objects as text boxes and put as much text in them (and on each arrow) as is necessary to be clear (see {{cite:786a7e889483b9dc754641133ea5f1fab96d72f5}}). For example, we might draw the indexing category for directed graphs in either of the following two ways: {{formula:b35830be-5be7-4a34-b7b6-fb8f3e01b3b7}} When in the typographical context of inline text we are discussing an object that has been elsewhere displayed as a textbox (such as an edge), we may represent it with corner symbols (e.g. as {{formula:b659f44d-acca-40bd-b5e0-dd08f8cad13d}} an edge{{formula:e8e36bed-69d4-4d2b-b168-c84d0a014fdb}} ) to avoid various spacing issues that can arise. Given two categories, there are generally many functors from one to the other; however, if the objects and arrows are labeled coherently, there are many fewer functors that roughly respect the labelings. We will usually be explicit when defining functors, but we will also take care that our functors respect labeling to the extent possible. I would like to thank Henrik Forssell, Peter Gates, Rich Haney, Eric Prud'hommeaux, and Emily Riehl for many useful discussions. The basic mantra is that a database schema is a small category {{formula:aea49ad8-3f32-4d23-aea5-c97d39830396}} and an instance is a functor {{formula:9621a244-df3e-4296-a2ac-3d39b442ba7f}} , where {{formula:5ba5f5fc-25bc-4e78-b84c-220c0f07e2ee}} is the category of sets.If one prefers, {{formula:a32f377f-927c-4fb7-b81b-a05e9650cb54}} can be replaced by the category of finite sets or by the category Types for some {{formula:f6809178-478e-49bd-b16d-41b43ef01e34}} -calculus. To recall these ideas, we take liberally from {{cite:46bb68c307a45c3dbb22a4518bf779e6dab6e590}}, though more details and clarification are given there. Readers who are familiar with the basic setup and data migration functors can skip to Section REF . In {{cite:46bb68c307a45c3dbb22a4518bf779e6dab6e590}} a category {{formula:80834321-9c4e-4fe1-8b5b-2b47b936e156}} of categorical schemas and translations is defined and an equivalence of categories {{formula:e3125982-5b10-454f-aaef-3db7925fefa8}} is proved, where {{formula:b8a28364-0416-4ae7-8990-c8ae78d32b0a}} is the category of small categories. The difference between {{formula:354811df-30b0-44b2-bdf2-da33f5729b15}} and {{formula:5c4e993e-916e-45c3-b239-f0326e42da35}} is that an object of the former is a chosen presentation of a category, by generators and relations. Given the equivalence (REF ), we can and do elide the difference between schemas and small categories. Roughly, a schema {{formula:43a51b45-f7ca-42c5-bfc7-235198c9d33a}} consists of a graph {{formula:dc9e6ff1-5499-42d9-b46b-9c8d5ecf1aff}} together with an equivalence relation on the set of paths of {{formula:5726bce8-b8fc-45cb-9484-85d1d6f4b5ef}} . Each object {{formula:01a96570-4b10-4d25-91ad-b9f085d85b33}} represents a table (or more precisely the ID column of a table), and each arrow {{formula:efc219aa-e787-4203-b1ea-4f58770e2d55}} emanating from {{formula:c4c4fc62-e5cd-4ff3-9b7e-99612d35bbb8}} represents a column of table {{formula:02590aa5-80b7-4e0f-b7e5-2999ed5f0ab8}} , taking values in the ID column of table {{formula:ce146be5-84dd-4cee-99f2-a4d3e459fd22}} . An example should clarify the ideas. Example 2.1.1 As a typical database example, consider the bookkeeping necessary to run a department store. We keep track of a set of employees and a set of departments. For each employee {{formula:4aedbd0c-db25-4dcb-b5d5-fcafe3fa9acf}} , we keep track of the first name of {{formula:5ebb4a8a-003d-4c2a-9add-1547d1056373}} , which is a FirstNameString, the last name of {{formula:83687a76-6937-4643-bb24-61e4bfd88539}} , which is a LastNameString, the manager of {{formula:29c99b32-277d-4fbc-8b45-20f2e9bfea3e}} , which is an Employee, and the department that {{formula:8c0ad384-3a14-4568-9c2f-cdeb270232c8}} works in, which is a Department. For each department {{formula:9e643424-de82-440c-8c94-b466df0bd644}} , we keep track of the name of {{formula:e0b4814a-3693-48c1-bfbc-a48b7f15aecf}} , which is a DepartmentNameString, and the secretary of {{formula:598caae3-1f40-4499-89fa-e446c9e6c37a}} , which is an Employee. Suppose further that we make the following two rules. For every employee {{formula:ced29128-e86d-4cbc-9ad9-310a7b24e3a0}} , the manager of {{formula:ea134d24-a8b5-4fc1-956f-2fe3c9b0e32b}} works in the same department that {{formula:4338bce8-c007-49fe-a990-29fed4cbf05c}} works in. For every department {{formula:1dce9b1c-29ef-4ed1-b02d-72bd2eec22dc}} , the secretary of {{formula:14f7e9b9-25f3-49bf-82b5-4b785a650bd8}} works in department {{formula:9952e009-9897-41e1-b14b-34316d6c13fd}} . This is all captured neatly, with nothing left out and nothing else added, by the category presented below: {{formula:f5fd1ffc-44bc-4e27-a00b-241994563928}} The underlined statements at the top indicate pairs of commutative (i.e. equivalent) paths; each path is indicated by its source object followed by the sequence of arrows that composes it. The objects, arrows, and equivalences in {{formula:7705ecfa-cbab-4719-aff3-8763ae711b3f}} correspond to the tables, columns, and rules laid out at the beginning of this example. The collection of data on a schema is typically presented in table form. Display (REF ) shows how a database with schema {{formula:43a5f81c-e0e5-4755-8406-7af8647021c7}} might look at a particular moment in time. {{formula:702d18a0-d918-4ce0-80a0-cf2b65506ace}} {{formula:bde813fc-2cfc-47ea-883c-92ec92747d9a}} Every table has an ID column, and in every table each cell references a cell in the ID column of some table. For example, cells in the secretary column of the Department table refer to cells in the ID column of the Employee table. Finally, one checks that Rule 1 and Rule 2 hold. For example, let {{formula:0b2cb04d-d7ad-4c4d-8734-b782755240a0}} be Employee 101. He works in Department q10 and his manager is Employee 103. Employee 103 works in Department q10 as well, as required. The point is that the data in (REF ) conform precisely to the schema {{formula:429de942-b968-470b-865e-10138cef5c3c}} from Diagram (REF ). A set of tables that conforms to	0.9
cs/0610128	limitation	Some implementation issues were not addressed.	By a direct product {{cite:aa49b595a8cf654ccc092582befe47263fd8efa8}}, Hierarchical Bin Buffering and therefore the Ola approach can be generalized to the multidimensional case. Some implementation issues were not addressed. For example, many forms of buffering using finite-accuracy floating-point numbers are susceptible to significant numerical errors. Finally, the source code used for the production of this paper is freely available {{cite:cdac3b577396445090fa52fc9f277b0d703ee418}}.	0.9
